      <div type="clause" n="Iudex 1">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Iudex">Iudex</ref> Iudex 1</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Iudex 1" corresp="#wit-q">Omnis iudex iustus misericordiam et iudicium libret in omnibus, ut inprimis per rectam scientiam dicat emendationem secundum culpam et eam tamen admensuret propter indulgentiam.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="Iudex 1" corresp="#wit-g-a">Ælc man, þe riht demeð, he hæfð wæga on handa; ⁊ on ægðere sceale he byrþ rihtwisnesse ⁊ mildheortnesse: ærest for ðære rihtwisnesse he gereceð ðæs gyltes bote ⁊ þonne for ðære mildheortnesse he gemetegað þære scylde wíte.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">Jedermann, der richtigen Spruch ertheilt, der hat Wagschalen in der Hand; und in je einer Schale trägt er Gerechtigkeit und Barmherzigkeit: zuerst bestimmt er infolge der Gerechtigkeit Busse für die Verschuldung, und alsdann um der Barmherzigkeit willen ermässigt er die Strafe für die Schuld.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">Everyone who gives a right judgment holds scales in his hand; and in each scale he carries justice and mercy: first, by reason of justice, he fixes the compensation for the offence, and then, for mercy's sake, he moderates the penalty for the guilt.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#unattributed" corresp="#wit-g-a">Every man who judges justly holds a balance in his hand: on each scale he carries justice and mercy: first, for the sake of justice he decides the punishment for the guilty, and then for the sake of mercy he moderates the pain of the penalty.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#liebermann" n="474">Isidor, Sent. III 52.4</note>
        <note type="comparisonPrinted" source="#liebermann">Isidor, Sent. III 52, 4</note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="383"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:Af_El-to-40">Af El 40–46</ref> <quote>cf. Af Int. 40 – 46, 49:5–6, I Ew Pr., 2 – 2:1, II Ew 1:2 – 2, 8; II As 3:2, 25 – 25:1, V As 1:2–5, VI As 11</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="383"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:Af_El-to-49-5">Af El 49.5–6</ref> <quote>cf. Af Int. 40 – 46, 49:5–6, I Ew Pr., 2 – 2:1, II Ew 1:2 – 2, 8; II As 3:2, 25 – 25:1, V As 1:2–5, VI As 11</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="383"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:IEw_Prol">I Ew Prol.</ref> <quote>cf. Af Int. 40 – 46, 49:5–6, I Ew Pr., 2 – 2:1, II Ew 1:2 – 2, 8; II As 3:2, 25 – 25:1, V As 1:2–5, VI As 11</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="383"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:IEw_2">I Ew 2–2.1</ref> <quote>cf. Af Int. 40 – 46, 49:5–6, I Ew Pr., 2 – 2:1, II Ew 1:2 – 2, 8; II As 3:2, 25 – 25:1, V As 1:2–5, VI As 11</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="383"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:IIEw_1-2">II Ew 1.2–2</ref> <quote>cf. Af Int. 40 – 46, 49:5–6, I Ew Pr., 2 – 2:1, II Ew 1:2 – 2, 8; II As 3:2, 25 – 25:1, V As 1:2–5, VI As 11</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="383"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:IIEw_8">II Ew 8</ref> <quote>cf. Af Int. 40 – 46, 49:5–6, I Ew Pr., 2 – 2:1, II Ew 1:2 – 2, 8; II As 3:2, 25 – 25:1, V As 1:2–5, VI As 11</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="383"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:IIAs_3-2">II As 3.2</ref> <quote>cf. Af Int. 40 – 46, 49:5–6, I Ew Pr., 2 – 2:1, II Ew 1:2 – 2, 8; II As 3:2, 25 – 25:1, V As 1:2–5, VI As 11</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="383"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:IIAs_25">II As 25–25.1</ref> <quote>cf. Af Int. 40 – 46, 49:5–6, I Ew Pr., 2 – 2:1, II Ew 1:2 – 2, 8; II As 3:2, 25 – 25:1, V As 1:2–5, VI As 11</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="383"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:VAs_1-2">V As 1.2–5</ref> <quote>cf. Af Int. 40 – 46, 49:5–6, I Ew Pr., 2 – 2:1, II Ew 1:2 – 2, 8; II As 3:2, 25 – 25:1, V As 1:2–5, VI As 11</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="383"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:VIAs_11">VI As 11</ref> <quote>cf. Af Int. 40 – 46, 49:5–6, I Ew Pr., 2 – 2:1, II Ew 1:2 – 2, 8; II As 3:2, 25 – 25:1, V As 1:2–5, VI As 11</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="383"><label>main likenesses</label> <ref target="clause:IIIEg_1-1">III Eg 1.1–3</ref> <quote>the main likenesses are with III Edgar [n. III Eg 1:1 – 3]</quote></note>
        <note type="editorial">The Latin is the Quadripartitus; Liebermann's Old English is from G (Cotton Nero A I, f. 48, after Romscot, same hand c. 1060). Liebermann dates Iudex c. 980–c. 1050; it draws on Isidore, Sententiae III 52–54 (here 52.4). From 8 on the Quadripartitus probably used the source of the OE directly, Liebermann thinks.</note>
      </div>
